#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""helloworld — publish an allowlisted squash of a private repo to its public twin, the privacy
scan as the gate.

    helloworld check   <repo> [--ref REF]   build the public tree, scan it, print; never pushes
    helloworld publish <repo> [-m MSG]      the same at origin/main, then the repo's name typed
                                            back, one commit, one push; the owner's terminal only
    helloworld publish --pr <repo> [-m TITLE] [--topic T] [--note TEXT]
                                            the same tree as a pull request on the public repo,
                                            from a claude/publish/* branch; the agent's verb;
                                            the owner's merge is the publish

Was `opensource` until 2026-10-18; the old names still read (below). The design ("don't make it
too complex to update"), what the gate cannot see, and the post-mortem a hard push on a public
repo owes: the module's README.md.

A repo opts in with these files, read from the ref being published (what is reviewed is what is
used), never from the working tree:
  .helloworld/allowlist.txt   one path per line, file by file: no globs, no folders
  .helloworld/exemptions.txt  `<file>:<exact string>` per line — text already public (the film's
                              own words, the public contact, a notice's author) and nowhere else
  .helloworld/remote          the public repo's URL
  .helloworld/identities      the addresses that may stand in the public history, one per line
                              (optional: the machine's git identity stands in, with a warning)
`#` lines and blank lines are skipped in both lists. Nothing under `.helloworld/` is ever published.
A ref without `.helloworld/` but with the old `.opensource/` is read from there, with a printed
note to `git mv` it; neither folder is ever published.

The private word list (household names, the house, the employer, accounts, login dirs, machine
names; one per line, case-blind) is a file outside every repo, as a brand's blocklist is:
$HELLOWORLD_WORDLIST, else $OPENSOURCE_WORDLIST (the old name), else helloworld/wordlist.txt in
the regatta config dir ($REGATTA_CONFIG, else .config/regatta in the home folder), else the old
default, .config/opensource/wordlist.txt in the home folder. Without it the gate refuses to run:
a scan that cannot see the household is not a gate. An entry of 5 or more characters matches
anywhere, inside `harbourview_home`, `MyLaptopSetup` or `mylaptop2` too; a shorter one only
between non-letters (`Ed`, not `Fred`); the spaces in a two-word name match any whitespace, a line
wrap included. Erring towards a false finding is the safe side: an exemption clears one. A
trailing `# comment` on an entry is stripped (`Harbourview  # the house` is Harbourview), and a `#`
left inside an entry is refused: it would never match.

The scan, over every file's whole text and its path (P2 of the design, extended, and widened by
the landing review of 2026-10-09 and the PR mode's review the same day): machine paths, `~/` and
`$HOME/` paths, Drive and scratchpad paths, LAN addresses (RFC 1918, Tailscale's 100.64/10,
link-local, IPv6 unique-local and link-local) and LAN host names (`.local`, `.lan`, `.home.arpa`,
a tailnet's `.ts.net`), e-mail addresses, session links and `Claude-Session`, anything embedded as
`data:<type>/<subtype>[;…];base64,`, credential shapes (vendor keys, JWTs, Telegram bot tokens, key-like
assignments; always printed masked), and the word list. Every pattern runs on the raw text. `CREDITS.yaml` at
the tree's root is the consent list: a word-list entry that equals a name under its `people:`
(case-blind) is dropped from the list, and nothing else changes — a credit hides no address, no
path and no other name. A `lines:` or `fix:` key in a cut list (a .yaml, .yml or .json under a
folder named `edl`) is refused and no exemption reaches it: those are the people's spoken words,
kept in sidecars under $WORK (E1). A file that is not UTF-8 is refused. An exemption clears only
the findings that lie wholly inside an occurrence of its exact string, in the file it names; one
whose string is not in that file, or that clears nothing, is refused, and each one used is printed
with the count it cleared, so a string that left the film leaves the list (R5). Run inside an
agent's shell (CLAUDECODE set), private words and addresses print masked: the transcript is read
again by later sessions.

How the tree is built: a temporary index holding only the allowlisted blobs of the ref, written
as one tree (`write-tree`) — the same tree a detached worktree and
`read-tree` produce, with nothing checked out and nothing to clean up. `check` fetches origin and
the public main's objects, writes that tree object, and changes no ref of its own; it never
commits and never pushes.

`publish` refuses inside an agent's shell (CLAUDECODE set) and without a terminal on stdin. That
stops accidents, not intent: a shell that unsets the variable and fakes a terminal gets through
(tests/helloworld.sh does exactly that to prove the publish path), the same trade as
GIT_PUSH_OK; a PreToolUse deny on `helloworld publish` would be the next layer. It refuses a
remote that is one of the private repo's own remotes (the allowlist-only tree would fast-forward
the private main). It fetches origin, builds and scans origin/main (never a branch: what goes
public has landed), scans and shows both identities the commit carries (dates in UTC, so no time
zone goes public), shows the diff-stat against the public repo's main, asks for the public repo's
owner/name, commits the tree and pushes it as main with GIT_PUSH_OK=1 (the repo's own pre-push
guard lets it through). The first publish is an orphan;
every later one is a child of the public main as fetched, so it fast-forwards: branch protection
(applied after the first push) forbids a force-push, and a credit merged on GitHub stays in the
public history, while the diff-stat shows whether this tree keeps it. The push is read back with
ls-remote before it counts. The record of each publish goes to
<git dir>/helloworld/PUBLISHED.md (<git dir>/opensource/ while only that exists), private and
outside the working tree (not .helloworld/: a file there would leave the primary main dirty after
every publish and need its own pull request); the next run's diff base is the public repo itself, which is the ground truth.

`publish --pr` is the route that needs no typed name (the owner, 2026-10-09: "publishing through a
PR is the right way"; auto-merge stays off): the same tree, built and scanned at origin/main, becomes
one commit on top of the public main, dated in UTC like publish's, and goes up as a branch with a
pull request against main. The owner's merge on GitHub is the publish, so an agent may run it, and
nothing here ever writes the public main. Everything a PR shows is public the moment it is
pushed, drafts included, so the title (the commit's message too), the body, the branch name, the
commit's identities and the gh login that opens the PR are scanned with the files, against the
whole word list (a credit is consent for the published tree, not for this tool's own words), and
nothing is pushed unless all of them pass — bar a login that already authored a merged PR in a
public repo of the target owner, which is public already (a failed search scans it). The body is built from the scan, not copied from this
tool's output: no local path, no private sha, the exemptions as files and counts, never their
strings, and removed files counted, not named (a take-back must not be refused for naming what
it takes back).
  The branch is claude/publish/<date>-<topic>, not claude/<host>/…: machine names are on the
private word list. The push runs from a clone of the public repo kept in the private repo's git
dir (<git dir>/helloworld/public, origin = .helloworld/remote), with the wharf module's
templates/code/git-hooks/pre-push (found through the core; without it --pr refuses) copied into
the clone's own hooks folder (a set core.hooksPath is refused: git would
never run it) and GIT_PUSH_OK and VSCODE_GIT_COMMAND stripped, so the push reaches claude/* only.
The commit is made in the private repo under refs/helloworld/pr, fetched into the clone by that
ref — which carries only the objects the commit reaches, the allowlisted blobs and the public
history — and the ref is deleted again, so no ref of the gate's own outlives a run. Auto-merge is
off by refusal: a workflow on the public main or in the proposed tree whose name or content says
auto-merge (`gh pr merge`, `merge --auto`, enablePullRequestAutoMerge, `pulls.merge`, a
`/pulls/N/merge` call), or a Mergify, Kodiak or Bulldozer config, stops the run — a text check of
the house shapes, not of GitHub's own settings, since a ready claude/* PR would land itself. The same tree already on a
claude/publish/* branch is not proposed twice while that branch's PR is open; without an open PR
the run exits 2 with the command to open one. A first publish has no main to be a pull request
against, so it stays `publish`.

The public repo's history is scanned too (the 220-film incident, 2026-10-10: GitHub authored a
squash commit with the merging account's private address, and the gate saw only the tree and its own
commit). `check` fetches every public branch and tag (the one fetch that also gives the diff base's
objects; no ref of ours moves) and reads every commit reachable from them as raw objects: author and
committer, the tagger of any mergetag header, the whole message; each annotated tag (a tag of a tag
one level at a time, by its object header): tagger and message; every ref name. Addresses and names
go through the e-mail pattern and the word list, ref names and messages through the word list too.
Passing addresses: those in .helloworld/identities (one per line, from the reviewed ref; without
the file, the identity this machine's git would publish as, and a WARNING line), `noreply@github.com`
as committer or in text, and `[<id>+]<login-or-name[bot]>@users.noreply.github.com`; any other
address is a finding labelled `history <sha> <field>`, masked in an agent's shell like the tree's.
With the file, publish and publish --pr refuse when git's own address is not in it, unless it is a
person's GitHub noreply address (a bot's still needs its line). A time-zone offset other than +0000 is a printed warning only. Such a commit can only go by a hard push
(the owner's; README.md). A public repo that cannot be reached leaves the history unread:
check exits 2 (1 if the tree also has findings), as publish does. publish --pr also reads the
public repo's merge settings (gh api repos/<o>/<r>) and refuses unless squash and merge commits
are off: a squash commit is authored by the merging account's own address.

Exit 0 clean (or published), 1 refused by the scan, 2 a setup or usage error. Stdlib, Python 3.9.
"""
import argparse
import datetime as dt
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
from pathlib import Path


sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib"))
import find_core  # noqa: E402

sys.path.insert(0, find_core.hooks())
import common  # noqa: E402

CONF = ".helloworld"
OLD_CONF = ".opensource"   # the name until 2026-10-18; read when a ref has no .helloworld/
CONFS = (CONF, OLD_CONF)
PR_REF = "refs/helloworld/pr"
PR_PREFIX = "claude/publish/"
GUARD_REL = Path("templates") / "code" / "git-hooks" / "pre-push"


def guard_path():
    """The pre-push guard the public clone runs: the wharf module's template, else the old
    checkout's (`REGATTA_LEGACY`, the legacy file), else None — then --pr refuses (the design's
    G30). Through the core, never a path beside this file: the guard is wharf's."""
    for base in (common.module_path("wharf"), common.legacy_root()):
        if base and (base / GUARD_REL).is_file():
            return base / GUARD_REL
    return None

# (category, pattern, masked). Masked hits print their first two characters only.
PATTERNS = [
    ("machine path", re.compile(r"/(?:Users|home)/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+|/private/(?:tmp|var)/|/var/folders/|"
                                r"/Volumes/|[A-Za-z]:\\Users\\"), False),
    ("home path", re.compile(r"(?<![\w.~/])~/[\w.]|\$\{?HOME\}?/"), False),
    ("Drive or scratchpad path", re.compile(r"GoogleDrive-|CloudStorage|Shared drives|My Drive/|scratchpad|"
                                            r"\.claude/worktrees|/tmp/claude", re.I), False),
    # RFC 1918, Tailscale's CGNAT range (100.64/10: every tailnet address) and link-local.
    ("LAN address", re.compile(r"(?<![\d.])(?:10\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|192\.168\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|"
                               r"172\.(?:1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|"
                               r"100\.(?:6[4-9]|[7-9]\d|1[01]\d|12[0-7])\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|169\.254\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3})"
                               r"(?!\.?\d)"), False),
    # IPv6 unique-local (fc00::/7, Tailscale's fd7a:…) and link-local (fe80::/10).
    ("LAN address", re.compile(r"(?<![\w:])(?:f[cd][0-9a-f]{2}|fe[89ab][0-9a-f]):(?:[0-9a-f]{0,4}:){1,6}[0-9a-f]{0,4}"
                               r"(?![\w:])", re.I), False),
    # A name only the LAN or the tailnet resolves, ended by anything but a word character, a call
    # or a longer name (review 2026-10-09: an allow-list of end characters let `tower.local` in
    # backticks, <http://nas.lan> and `@host.local>` through). `threading.local()` is not one;
    # a bare `threading.local` or `settings.local` is, and an exemption clears it.
    ("LAN host name", re.compile(r"(?<![\w.-])[\w-]+(?:\.[\w-]+)*\.(?:local|lan|home\.arpa|ts\.net)"
                                 r"(?![\w(-])(?!\.\w)", re.I), False),
    ("e-mail address", re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9-]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9-]+)*\.[A-Za-z]{2,}"), False),
    ("session link", re.compile(r"claude\.ai/(?:code/)?session|Claude-Session|session_0[0-9A-Za-z]{10,}"), False),
    # A picture, a sound or a document inside a text file: the gate reads text, so whatever it
    # holds passes unseen (review 2026-10-09: parameters before ;base64, and audio, too).
    ("embedded data", re.compile(r"data:[\w.+-]+/[\w.+-]+(?:;[\w.+=-]+)*;base64,", re.I), False),
    # Vendor shapes (OpenAI incl. sk-proj, Anthropic, Stripe, Porkbun's pk1_/sk1_, GitHub, AWS,
    # Google, Slack, a JWT such as Home Assistant's long-lived token, a Telegram bot token), then
    # the generic rule: a quoted value of 8+ characters, any symbols; or an unquoted one that does
    # not go on into a call or an attribute (`token = os.environ.get(…)` is code, not a secret).
    # Every vendor shape sits behind one left edge (review 2026-10-09: `task1_processing` and
    # `risk_test_cases` hit as sk1_ and sk_test_ inside an identifier).
    ("credential", re.compile(r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9])(?:sk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}|sk-ant-[\w-]{8,}|[sr]k_(?:live|test)_\w{16,}|[ps]k1_\w{16,}|"
                              r"gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}|github_pat_\w{20,}|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|AIza[0-9A-Za-z_-]{30,}|"
                              r"xox[abprs]-[\w-]{10,}|eyJ[\w-]{10,}\.eyJ[\w-]{10,}\.[\w-]{10,})|"
                              r"-----BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----|(?<!\d)\d{8,10}:AA[\w-]{30,}|"
                              r"(?i:(?:api[_-]?key|secret|token|passw(?:or)?d)\w*['\"]?\s*[:=]\s*"
                              r"(?:['\"][^'\"\s]{8,}['\"]|[A-Za-z0-9_\-+/=.]{8,}(?![\w(\[.\-+/=])))"), True),
]
# A key, quoted or bare, at the start of a block line (after any run of "- ") or inside a flow
# mapping or sequence; quoted text that says "fix:" is not a key.
EDL_KEY = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:-\s+)*[\"']?(?:lines|fix)[\"']?\s*:|[{,\[]\s*[\"']?(?:lines|fix)[\"']?\s*:")
# "IMG_5814@496.json" is a transcript's file name, not an address (P2 of the design counted one):
# digits after the @ and a file suffix. company.sh and example.md stay addresses.
FILE_NAME_AT = re.compile(r"@\d+\.(?:json|ya?ml|md|txt|csv|tsv|srt|vtt|mp4|mov|m4a|wav|mp3|jpe?g|png|heic|pdf)$", re.I)
CREDIT_NAME = re.compile(r"(?:^|[{,\s])name:\s*(?:\"([^\"]+)\"|'([^']+)'|([^,}\n#]+?))\s*(?:[,}#]|$)")
MASK_IN_AGENT = {"private word", "e-mail address"}
# A workflow that lands a PR by itself, by its name or by what it runs (review 2026-10-09:
# auto-merge.yml, or a pr.yml running `gh pr merge --auto`, passed a name-only check), and
# Mergify, which needs no workflow at all.
AUTOMERGE = re.compile(r"auto[-_]?merge|merge\s+--auto|enablePullRequestAutoMerge|gh\s+pr\s+merge|"
                       r"pulls\.merge|/pulls/[^/\s]+/merge", re.I)
MERGIFY = {".mergify.yml", ".github/mergify.yml", ".mergify/config.yml", ".kodiak.toml", ".github/.kodiak.toml",
           ".bulldozer.yml"}


class Refused(Exception):
    """A setup error: printed, exit 2."""


# No replace refs, ever: a refs/replace/* in a clone would show this gate another tree and other
# commits than the ones that go public.
GIT_ENV = {"GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT": "0", "GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS": "1"}


def git(repo, *args, check=True, env=None, input=None):
    e = dict(os.environ, **{**GIT_ENV, **(env or {})})
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *args], capture_output=True, text=True, env=e, input=input)
    if check and r.returncode:
        raise Refused(f"git {' '.join(args[:3])}: {(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()}")
    return r


def blob(repo, sha):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), "cat-file", "blob", sha],
                          capture_output=True, check=True, env=dict(os.environ, **GIT_ENV)).stdout


def entries(repo, ref):
    """Every file of the ref: path -> (mode, sha)."""
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), "ls-tree", "-r", "-z", "--full-tree", ref],
                         capture_output=True, check=True, env=dict(os.environ, **GIT_ENV)).stdout.decode()
    files = {}
    for rec in filter(None, out.split("\0")):
        meta, path = rec.split("\t", 1)
        mode, kind, sha = meta.split()
        files[path] = (mode, kind, sha)
    return files


def lines_of(text):
    return [s for s in (l.rstrip("\r") for l in text.splitlines()) if s.strip() and not s.lstrip().startswith("#")]


def conf_dir(files):
    """The ref's opt-in folder: .helloworld/, else the old .opensource/, else .helloworld/ (whose
    absence read_conf then names)."""
    for c in CONFS:
        if any(f.startswith(c + "/") for f in files):
            return c
    return CONF


def read_conf(repo, ref, files, name, conf=CONF):
    path = f"{conf}/{name}"
    if path not in files:
        raise Refused(f"{ref} has no {path} — the repo has not opted in")
    return blob(repo, files[path][2]).decode()


def wordlist_path():
    """The first that is set or exists: $HELLOWORLD_WORDLIST, $OPENSOURCE_WORDLIST, the config
    dir's helloworld/wordlist.txt, the old default; a variable that is set wins even when its file
    is missing, so a typo is refused rather than passed over. The same for the config dir's entry:
    one that exists but is no readable file (a broken link, a Drive file not yet downloaded) is
    refused, never passed over for the old list, which may lack the newest words."""
    for v in ("HELLOWORLD_WORDLIST", "OPENSOURCE_WORDLIST"):
        if os.environ.get(v):
            return Path(os.path.expanduser(os.environ[v]))
    new = common.CONFIG / "helloworld" / "wordlist.txt"
    old = Path.home() / ".config" / "opensource" / "wordlist.txt"
    return old if old.is_file() and not os.path.lexists(new) else new


def wordlist():
    p = wordlist_path()
    if not p.is_file():
        raise Refused(f"no private word list at {p} — household, machine and account names cannot be "
                      "checked, so nothing is scanned (one word or name per line)")
    # A trailing comment is stripped, not matched: `Harbourview  # the house` must still find
    # Harbourview. A `#` that is still inside an entry would never match anything: refused.
    words = [re.sub(r"\s+#.*$", "", w).strip() for w in lines_of(p.read_text())]
    odd = [w for w in words if "#" in w]
    if odd:
        raise Refused(f"the private word list {p} has an entry with '#' in it — a comment goes on its own "
                      "line or after a space")
    if not words:
        raise Refused(f"the private word list {p} is empty")
    return p, words


def norm(name):
    return " ".join(name.split()).casefold()


def words_regex(words, credited=()):
    """The word list, one pattern per entry (so overlapping entries are each found), less the
    entries a person credited themselves with. Built from the original spellings: casefold turns
    "ß" into "ss", and the entry would no longer match itself."""
    drop = {norm(c) for c in credited}
    out = []
    for w in dict.fromkeys(w for w in words if norm(w) not in drop):
        body = r"\s+".join(map(re.escape, w.split()))
        out.append(re.compile(body if len(w) >= 5 else r"(?<![A-Za-z])" + body + r"(?![A-Za-z])", re.I))
    return out


def mask_words(text, words_rx):
    """Inside an agent's shell, every private word in a printed line — a path, a machine-path hit."""
    if not os.environ.get("CLAUDECODE"):
        return text
    for rx in words_rx:
        text = rx.sub(lambda m: m.group()[:2] + "…", text)
    return text


def credits(text):
    """Names under CREDITS.yaml's top-level `people:`, block or flow style; comments skipped."""
    names, inside = [], False
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if re.match(r"\S", line) and not line.startswith("#"):
            inside = line.startswith("people:")
        if not inside or line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        for m in CREDIT_NAME.finditer(line):
            n = next(g for g in m.groups() if g).strip()
            if n:
                names.append(n)
    return names


def allowlist(text, files):
    paths, bad = [], []
    for raw in lines_of(text):
        p = raw.strip()
        if re.search(r"[*?\[]", p):
            bad.append(f"{p}: a glob — list files one by one")
        elif p.startswith("/") or ".." in p.split("/"):
            bad.append(f"{p}: not a path inside the repo")
        elif any(p == c or p.startswith(c + "/") for c in CONFS):
            bad.append(f"{p}: the gate's own lists are never published")
        elif p in paths:
            bad.append(f"{p}: listed twice")
        elif p not in files:
            under = any(f.startswith(p.rstrip("/") + "/") for f in files)
            bad.append(f"{p}: {'a folder — list its files' if under else 'not in the tree'}")
        elif files[p][1] != "blob":
            bad.append(f"{p}: a {files[p][1]}, not a file")
        else:
            paths.append(p)
    return paths, bad


def exemptions(text):
    ex = []
    for raw in lines_of(text):
        f, sep, s = raw.partition(":")
        if not sep or not s:
            raise Refused(f"exemption '{raw}' is not <file>:<exact string>")
        ex.append((f.strip(), s))
    return ex


def is_edl(path):
    parts = path.split("/")
    return "edl" in (d.lower() for d in parts[:-1]) and path.lower().endswith((".yaml", ".yml", ".json"))


def shown(cat, text, masked):
    if masked or (cat in MASK_IN_AGENT and os.environ.get("CLAUDECODE")):
        return text[:2] + "…"
    return " ".join(text.split())


def text_hits(text, words_rx, skip=(), only=None):
    """Every finding in text as (start, end, category, shown), whole text at once; `only` limits
    the categories looked for."""
    out = []
    for cat, rx, masked in PATTERNS + [("private word", rx, False) for rx in words_rx]:
        if cat in skip or (only is not None and cat not in only):
            continue
        for m in rx.finditer(text):
            if cat == "e-mail address" and FILE_NAME_AT.search(m.group()):
                continue
            out.append((m.start(), m.end(), cat, shown(cat, m.group(), masked)))
    return out


def line_of(text, pos):
    return text.count("\n", 0, pos) + 1


def scan(repo, paths, files, ex, words, message=None, idents=(), meta=()):
    """Hits as (where, category, shown text); exemptions used as (file, string, cleared); bad exemptions.
    `meta` is the text a pull request makes public beside the files — (where, text, skip) — scanned
    whole against the entire word list: no exemption and no credit reaches it."""
    hits, used, bad = [], [], []
    texts = {}
    for p in paths:
        try:
            texts[p] = blob(repo, files[p][2]).decode("utf-8")
        except UnicodeDecodeError:
            hits.append((p, "not UTF-8 text", "the gate reads text only"))
    words_rx = words_regex(words, credits(texts.get("CREDITS.yaml", "")))
    for f, s in ex:
        if f not in texts:
            bad.append(f"{f}:{s} — {f} is not published")
        elif s not in texts[f]:
            bad.append(f"{f}:{s} — the string is not in {f}")
    for p, raw in texts.items():
        if is_edl(p):
            for n, line in enumerate(raw.splitlines(), 1):
                m = EDL_KEY.search(line)
                if m:  # the key only: the line itself is the words
                    hits.append((f"{p}:{n}", "people's words in a cut list", m.group().strip(" -{,[")))
        spans = [(f, s, [(m.start(), m.end()) for m in re.finditer(re.escape(s), raw)]) for f, s in ex if f == p]
        cleared = {(f, s): 0 for f, s, _ in spans}
        for start, end, cat, txt in text_hits(raw, words_rx):
            cover = next(((f, s) for f, s, occ in spans if any(a <= start and end <= b for a, b in occ)), None)
            if cover:
                cleared[cover] += 1
            else:
                hits.append((f"{p}:{line_of(raw, start)}", cat, txt))
        for (f, s), c in cleared.items():
            if c:
                used.append((f, s, c))
            elif s in raw:
                bad.append(f"{f}:{s} — clears no finding in {f}")
        hits += [(f"{p} (its path)", cat, txt) for _, _, cat, txt in text_hits(p, words_rx)]
    if message is not None:
        hits += [(f"commit message:{line_of(message, a)}", cat, txt) for a, _, cat, txt in text_hits(message, words_rx)]
    for label, ident in idents:  # the address is the identity the owner chose to publish; the rest is scanned
        hits += [(f"commit {label}", cat, txt) for _, _, cat, txt in text_hits(ident, words_rx, skip={"e-mail address"})]
    every_word = words_regex(words)
    for where, text, skip in meta:
        hits += [(f"{where}:{line_of(text, a)}", cat, txt) for a, _, cat, txt in text_hits(text, every_word, skip)]
    return hits, used, bad


def public_name(remote):
    """owner/name — the last segment alone is the same for someone/film and their-org/film."""
    return "/".join(url_key(remote).split("/")[-2:])


# GitHub's own committer on a squash, a merge or a web edit; the committer field only, and in text.
GITHUB_COMMITTER = "noreply@github.com"
# <id>+<login>@users.noreply.github.com, the older <login>@users.noreply.github.com, and a bot's
# <id>+<name>[bot]@users.noreply.github.com: addresses GitHub makes up, never a person's own.
NOREPLY = re.compile(r"^(?:\d+\+)?[A-Za-z0-9-]+(?:\[bot\])?@users\.noreply\.github\.com$", re.I)
IDENT = re.compile(r"^(.*?)\s*<([^<>]*)>\s*(?:(\d+)\s+([+-]\d{4}))?\s*$")
REMEDY = ("a commit like this can only go by a hard push, which is the owner's, and owes a post-mortem "
          "(helloworld's README.md, \"Post-mortem: a public repo needed a hard push\")")
NO_IDENTITIES = ("WARNING: identities from this machine's git config; add .helloworld/identities "
                 "(one address per line, read from the reviewed ref)")


def split_ident(text):
    """'Name <addr> 1700000000 -0700' as (name, addr, tz); tz is None without a timestamp, and
    without angle brackets the whole text is the name."""
    m = IDENT.match(text.strip())
    return (m.group(1), m.group(2).strip(), m.group(4)) if m else (text.strip(), "", None)


def publishing_emails(idents):
    """The addresses of the identity the owner chose to publish: what `publish` commits as."""
    return {split_ident(who)[1].lower() for _, who in idents if split_ident(who)[1]}


def identity_list(text):
    """.helloworld/identities: one address per line (`Name <addr>` is read as its address)."""
    out = set()
    for l in lines_of(text):
        m = IDENT.match(l.strip())
        out.add((m.group(2) if m else l.strip()).lower())
    return out


def address_ok(addr, allowed, committer=False):
    a = addr.lower()
    return a in allowed or bool(NOREPLY.match(a)) or (committer and a == GITHUB_COMMITTER)


def git_bytes(repo, *args, input=None):
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *args], capture_output=True, input=input, env=dict(os.environ, **GIT_ENV))
    if r.returncode:
        raise Refused(f"git {' '.join(args[:3])}: {r.stderr.decode(errors='replace').strip()}")
    return r.stdout


def read_objects(repo, shas):
    """sha -> (type, raw bytes), all through one `git cat-file --batch`."""
    shas = list(dict.fromkeys(shas))
    out = git_bytes(repo, "cat-file", "--batch", input=("\n".join(shas) + "\n").encode()) if shas else b""
    objs, i = {}, 0
    for sha in shas:
        j = out.index(b"\n", i)
        head, i = out[i:j].split(), j + 1
        if len(head) == 3:
            size = int(head[2])
            objs[sha] = (head[1].decode(), out[i:i + size])
            i += size + 1
    return objs


def split_object(raw):
    """A commit or tag object as ([(header, value)], message); a continuation line (one that starts
    with a space: gpgsig, mergetag) belongs to the header above it, joined by newlines."""
    head, _, body = raw.decode("utf-8", errors="replace").partition("\n\n")
    headers = []
    for line in head.split("\n"):
        if line.startswith(" ") and headers:
            headers[-1][1] += "\n" + line[1:]
        elif line:
            k, _, v = line.partition(" ")
            headers.append([k, v])
    return headers, body


def history_scan(repo, refs, allowed, words):
    """(findings, warnings) in the public repo's history: every commit reachable from every branch
    and tag, read as raw objects (no format string to fool). For each commit its author and
    committer, the tagger of any mergetag header, and the whole message; for each annotated tag,
    peeled one level at a time, its tagger and message; every ref name. Names and addresses go
    through the word list and the address pattern, whole messages likewise. Only the addresses in
    `allowed` (.helloworld/identities, else the publishing identity), GitHub's noreply@github.com
    as committer or in text, and *@users.noreply.github.com pass. A time-zone offset other than
    +0000 is a warning only (the owner's call, 2026-10-10): a rebase merge writes the merging account's own
    offset as the committer's. The commits GitHub makes itself (a squash or merge, a web edit, a
    repository's first commit) carry the merging account's own address, which this gate never wrote
    and the tree scan never sees."""
    rx = words_regex(words)
    hits, warns = [], []

    def text_findings(label, text, numbered=False):
        out = []
        for a, b, cat, txt in text_hits(text, rx, only={"e-mail address", "private word"}):
            if cat == "e-mail address" and (address_ok(text[a:b], allowed) or text[a:b].lower() == GITHUB_COMMITTER):
                continue
            out.append((f"{label}:{line_of(text, a)}" if numbered else label, cat, txt))
        return out

    def ident(label, line, committer=False):
        name, addr, tz = split_ident(line)
        out = text_findings(label, name)
        if addr:
            if address_ok(addr, allowed, committer):
                out += [(label, cat, txt) for _, _, cat, txt in text_hits(addr, rx, only={"private word"})]
            else:
                out.append((label, "e-mail address", shown("e-mail address", addr, False)))
        hits.extend(out)
        if tz and tz != "+0000":
            warns.append(f"{label}: time zone {tz} (warning)")

    for name, _ in refs:
        hits.extend((f"history ref {name}", cat, txt) for _, _, cat, txt in text_hits(name, rx, only={"private word"}))

    tips, seen, pending = [], set(), [sha for _, sha in refs]
    while pending:  # annotated tags one level at a time, through their object header
        nxt = []
        for sha, (kind, raw) in read_objects(repo, pending).items():
            if sha in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(sha)
            if kind == "commit":
                tips.append(sha)
            elif kind == "tag":
                headers, message = split_object(raw)
                for k, v in headers:
                    if k == "tagger":
                        ident(f"history {sha[:7]} tagger", v)
                    elif k == "object":
                        nxt.append(v.strip())
                hits.extend(text_findings(f"history {sha[:7]} tag message", message, True))
        pending = nxt
    if not tips:
        return hits, warns
    commits = git(repo, "rev-list", "--stdin", input="\n".join(tips) + "\n").stdout.split()
    for sha, (kind, raw) in read_objects(repo, commits).items():
        headers, message = split_object(raw)
        for k, v in headers:
            if k == "author":
                ident(f"history {sha[:7]} author", v)
            elif k == "committer":
                ident(f"history {sha[:7]} committer", v, committer=True)
            elif k == "mergetag":  # a signed tag merged in: its tagger and its words travel with it
                for line in v.split("\n"):
                    if line.startswith("tagger "):
                        ident(f"history {sha[:7]} mergetag tagger", line[7:])
                hits.extend(text_findings(f"history {sha[:7]} mergetag",
                                          "\n".join(l for l in v.split("\n") if not l.startswith("tagger ")), True))
        hits.extend(text_findings(f"history {sha[:7]} message", message, True))
    return hits, warns


def public_state(repo, remote):
    """The public repo as (main sha or None, [(ref name, object sha)] for every branch and tag). One ls-remote and one fetch, objects only (FETCH_HEAD; no ref of ours moves):
    the diff base and the history scan read the same objects. Refused when the remote is unreachable."""
    r = git(repo, "ls-remote", remote, "refs/heads/*", "refs/tags/*", check=False)
    if r.returncode:
        raise Refused(f"cannot reach {remote}: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
    refs = {}
    for l in r.stdout.splitlines():
        sha, _, name = l.partition("\t")
        if name and not name.endswith("^{}"):  # a peeled line repeats its tag; the walk follows object headers
            refs[name] = sha
    if refs:
        git(repo, "fetch", "-q", "--no-tags", remote, *refs)
    main = refs.get("refs/heads/main")
    if main:
        git(repo, "cat-file", "-e", f"{main}^{{commit}}")
    return main, list(refs.items())


def url_key(url):
    """One spelling per repo: git@host:o/r, https://host/o/r(.git) and a local path compare equal."""
    u = re.sub(r"^[a-z+]+://(?:[^@/]+@)?", "", url.strip())
    u = re.sub(r"^[^@/]+@", "", u)
    u = re.sub(r"^([^/:]+):\d+/", r"\1/", u).replace(":", "/")  # ssh://host:22/o/r is host/o/r
    return re.sub(r"\.git$", "", u.rstrip("/")).lower()


def own_remotes(repo):
    """Every fetch and push URL of the repo's remotes, insteadOf applied."""
    urls = []
    for name in git(repo, "remote", check=False).stdout.split():
        for extra in ([], ["--push"]):
            urls += git(repo, "remote", "get-url", "--all", *extra, name, check=False).stdout.splitlines()
    return urls


def build(repo, ref, check_only):
    """Everything up to the scan's verdict. Returns a dict for the caller to print and act on."""
    top = git(Path(repo).resolve(), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel", check=False)
    if top.returncode:
        raise Refused(f"{repo} is not a git repo")
    repo = Path(top.stdout.strip())  # a subfolder passed as <repo> is the repo, for every git call
    notes = []
    if ref == "origin/main":
        r = git(repo, "fetch", "-q", "origin", "main", check=False)
        if r.returncode:
            if not check_only:
                raise Refused(f"could not fetch origin: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]} — publish needs the current main")
            notes.append("origin not fetched; checking the origin/main this clone has")
    head = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", f"{ref}^{{commit}}", check=False).stdout.strip()
    if not head:
        raise Refused(f"no commit {ref} in {repo}")
    files = entries(repo, head)
    conf = conf_dir(files)
    if conf == OLD_CONF:
        notes.append(f"read from {OLD_CONF}/, the old name — git mv {OLD_CONF} {CONF} when next touched")
    remote = read_conf(repo, ref, files, "remote", conf).strip()
    if not remote:
        raise Refused(f"{conf}/remote is empty")
    if url_key(remote) in {url_key(u) for u in own_remotes(repo)}:
        raise Refused(f"{conf}/remote is {remote}, one of this repo's own remotes — the public twin is another repo")
    paths, bad_allow = allowlist(read_conf(repo, ref, files, "allowlist.txt", conf), files)
    if not paths and not bad_allow:  # an empty tree would delete every public file
        raise Refused(f"{conf}/allowlist.txt names no file — publishing it would empty the public repo")
    ex = exemptions(read_conf(repo, ref, files, "exemptions.txt", conf)) if f"{conf}/exemptions.txt" in files else []
    wl, words = wordlist()
    # The addresses that may stand in the public history: .helloworld/identities from the reviewed
    # ref; without it, the identity this machine's git would publish as (and a warning to add the file).
    idents = identities(repo)
    listed = f"{conf}/identities" in files
    allowed = identity_list(read_conf(repo, ref, files, "identities", conf)) if listed else publishing_emails(idents)
    if listed and not check_only:
        # Mine is the commit's author and committer; a person not on the list would sign the public
        # history with an address the gate has never been told may stand there. A GitHub noreply
        # address passes without a line, as in the history scan (address_ok) and the files' headers;
        # a bot's does not: the gate's own commit is a person's.
        if not all(a in allowed or (NOREPLY.match(a) and "[bot]@" not in a) for a in publishing_emails(idents)):
            raise Refused(f"the address git would commit as is not in {conf}/identities at {ref} — "
                          "add it there (reviewed), or set user.email to a listed one")
    return dict(repo=repo, ref=ref, head=head, files=files, remote=remote, paths=paths, bad_allow=bad_allow,
                ex=ex, wl=wl, words=words, notes=notes, idents=idents, allowed=allowed, listed=listed, warnings=[])


def history(b, refs):
    """The history findings; the time-zone warnings go to b for report to print."""
    hits, b["warnings"] = history_scan(b["repo"], refs, b["allowed"], b["words"])
    return hits


def write_tree(repo, paths, files):
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
        env = {"GIT_INDEX_FILE": str(Path(d) / "index")}
        info = "".join(f"{files[p][0]} {files[p][2]}\t{p}\n" for p in paths)
        git(repo, "update-index", "--add", "--index-info", env=env, input=info)
        return git(repo, "write-tree", env=env).stdout.strip()


def report(b, hits, used, bad_ex):
    when = git(b["repo"], "log", "-1", "--format=%cs", b["head"]).stdout.strip()
    wl_when = dt.date.fromtimestamp(b["wl"].stat().st_mtime).isoformat()
    print(f"helloworld — {b['repo']} at {b['ref']} ({b['head'][:7]}, {when})")
    print(f"  lists: allowlist {len(b['paths'])} files, exemptions {len(b['ex'])} (both from {b['ref']}); "
          f"word list {b['wl']} ({len(b['words'])} words, changed {wl_when})")
    print(f"  public: {b['remote']}")
    for n in b["notes"]:
        print(f"  note: {n}")
    if not b["listed"]:
        print(f"  {NO_IDENTITIES}")
    for w in b["warnings"]:
        print(f"  warning: {w}")
    rxs = words_regex(b["words"])
    for f, s, c in used:
        print(mask_words(f"  exemption used: {f}: \"{s}\" cleared {c}", rxs))
    problems = [f"allowlist: {x}" for x in b["bad_allow"]] + [f"exemption: {x}" for x in bad_ex]
    problems += [f"{w}: {cat} — {txt}" for w, cat, txt in hits]
    if problems:
        print(f"  REFUSED — {len(problems)} finding(s):")
        for p in problems:
            print(mask_words(f"    {p}", rxs))
        if any(w.startswith("history ") for w, _, _ in hits):
            print(f"  history: {REMEDY}")
    return not problems


def diff_lines(b, tree, parent):
    """The diff-stat of this tree against the public main; None when they are the same tree."""
    if git(b["repo"], "rev-parse", f"{parent}^{{tree}}").stdout.strip() == tree:
        return None
    return git(b["repo"], "diff", "--stat", parent, tree).stdout.rstrip().splitlines()


def diffstat(b, tree, parent):
    if not parent:
        print("  diff: the public repo has no main yet — this is the first publish")
        return True
    stat = diff_lines(b, tree, parent)
    if stat is None:
        print("  diff: nothing — the public main already has exactly this tree")
        return False
    print(f"  diff against the public main ({parent[:7]}):")
    print("\n".join(mask_words("    " + l, words_regex(b["words"])) for l in stat))
    return True


def identities(repo):
    """The two identities a commit made here would carry, without the timestamp."""
    out = []
    for k in ("author", "committer"):
        r = git(repo, "var", f"GIT_{k.upper()}_IDENT", check=False)  # none set: check still runs, and fails closed
        if not r.returncode:
            out.append((k, r.stdout.rsplit(" ", 2)[0]))
    return out


def author_trailer(idents):
    """`Signed-off-by: <the author identity>`, or None when git has no author identity.

    The public repo's DCO check wants every commit to carry one that matches its author's address,
    and the gate's own commits come from `git commit-tree`, which adds none. The identity is the
    `idents` author, read by `git var` in the repo and env the commit-tree runs in (never
    predicted). With none, commit-tree fails closed on its own."""
    author = dict(idents).get("author")
    return f"Signed-off-by: {author}" if author else None


def signed_off(message, idents):
    """The message plus its author's trailer after a blank line; one already there is not doubled."""
    trailer = author_trailer(idents)
    if not trailer or any(l.strip().lower() == trailer.lower() for l in message.splitlines()):
        return message
    return message.rstrip("\n") + "\n\n" + trailer


def log_path(repo):
    """<git dir>/helloworld/PUBLISHED.md; the old <git dir>/opensource/ while only it exists, so a
    clone that published before the rename keeps its log and its public clone in one place."""
    common_dir = Path(git(repo, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir").stdout.strip())
    new, old = common_dir / "helloworld", common_dir / "opensource"
    return (old if old.is_dir() and not new.exists() else new) / "PUBLISHED.md"


def cmd_check(args):
    b = build(args.repo, args.ref, check_only=True)
    hits, used, bad_ex = scan(b["repo"], b["paths"], b["files"], b["ex"], b["words"])
    # The same fetch the diff base uses also brings every branch and tag of the public repo, whose
    # commit identities (GitHub's squash commits among them) are scanned beside the tree.
    parent, unreachable = None, None
    try:
        parent, refs = public_state(b["repo"], b["remote"])
        hits += history(b, refs)
    except Refused as e:
        unreachable = e
    clean = report(b, hits, used, bad_ex)
    if unreachable:  # fails closed: a history that was not read is not a clean one
        print(f"  history: not scanned — {unreachable}")
        return 1 if not clean else 2
    if clean:
        tree = write_tree(b["repo"], b["paths"], b["files"])
        diffstat(b, tree, parent)
        print(f"  clean: {len(b['paths'])} files, tree {tree[:7]}. Nothing was pushed.")
    return 0 if clean else 1


def cmd_publish(args):
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDECODE"):
        raise Refused("publish runs in the owner's own terminal, never an agent's shell (CLAUDECODE is set); "
                      "`helloworld check` is the agent's verb")
    if not sys.stdin.isatty():
        raise Refused("publish asks for the repo's name typed back; stdin is not a terminal")
    b = build(args.repo, "origin/main", check_only=False)
    today = dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).date().isoformat()  # UTC, as the commit's dates
    message = args.message or f"Published {today}"
    idents = b["idents"]
    hits, used, bad_ex = scan(b["repo"], b["paths"], b["files"], b["ex"], b["words"], message=message, idents=idents)
    parent, refs = public_state(b["repo"], b["remote"])
    hits += history(b, refs)
    if not report(b, hits, used, bad_ex):
        return 1
    tree = write_tree(b["repo"], b["paths"], b["files"])
    last = log_path(b["repo"])
    rows = [l.split() for l in last.read_text().splitlines() if l.startswith("- ")] if last.is_file() else []
    # "- <date> from <private sha> <published sha> <remote>", this remote's rows only;
    # "- <date> pr <branch> <sha> <url>" rows are proposals, not publishes
    rows = [r for r in rows if len(r) == 6 and r[2] == "from" and r[5] == b["remote"]]
    logged = rows[-1][4] if rows else None
    if parent and logged and parent != logged:
        print(f"  note: the public main ({parent[:7]}) is not the last publish logged here ({logged[:7]}) — "
              "something was merged on GitHub; the diff below says whether this tree keeps it")
    if not diffstat(b, tree, parent):
        return 0
    name = public_name(b["remote"])
    print(f"  commit: \"{message}\", {'a child of ' + parent[:7] if parent else 'an orphan'}, dated in UTC")
    for k, who in idents:
        print(f"    {k}: {who}")
    print(f"    trailer: {author_trailer(idents) or 'none — git has no author identity, so the commit will fail'}")
    try:
        typed = input(f"Type the public repo's name ({name}) to push it to {b['remote']}: ").strip()
    except EOFError:
        typed = ""
    if typed.lower() != name:
        print("Not published: the name did not match.")
        return 2
    cargs = ["commit-tree", tree, "-m", signed_off(message, idents)] + (["-p", parent] if parent else [])
    utc = f"{int(time.time())} +0000"
    sha = git(b["repo"], *cargs, env={"GIT_AUTHOR_DATE": utc, "GIT_COMMITTER_DATE": utc}).stdout.strip()
    git(b["repo"], "push", "-q", b["remote"], f"{sha}:refs/heads/main", env={"GIT_PUSH_OK": "1"})
    seen = git(b["repo"], "ls-remote", b["remote"], "refs/heads/main").stdout.split()
    if not seen or seen[0] != sha:
        print(f"Pushed, but the public main reads {seen[0][:7] if seen else 'nothing'}, not {sha[:7]} — look before trusting it.")
        return 2
    log_row(b["repo"], f"- {today} from {b['head']} {sha} {b['remote']}")
    print(f"Published {sha[:7]} to {b['remote']} (read back). Logged in {last}.")
    return 0


def log_row(repo, row):
    last = log_path(repo)
    last.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with last.open("a") as fh:
        if fh.tell() == 0:
            fh.write("# What `helloworld publish` pushed from this clone: date, from (private), published.\n")
        fh.write(row + "\n")


def public_clone(b):
    """The private repo's clone of the public one, fetched now, its pre-push the wharf guard."""
    d = log_path(b["repo"]).parent / "public"
    if not (d / ".git").is_dir():
        d.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "clone", "-q", "--no-checkout", "--no-tags", b["remote"], str(d)],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, env=dict(os.environ, GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT="0"))
        if r.returncode:
            raise Refused(f"cannot clone {b['remote']}: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
    # An emptied or half-made public/.git is no repo, and git would walk up to the private
    # repo's own .git: every call below would then rewrite the private origin and push from it
    # (review 2026-10-09). The clone must be its own repo before anything runs in it.
    own = git(d, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir", check=False).stdout.strip()
    if not own or Path(own).resolve() != (d / ".git").resolve():
        raise Refused(f"{d} is not a clone of its own (git finds {own or 'nothing'}) — remove it and run again")
    git(d, "remote", "set-url", "origin", b["remote"])
    git(d, "fetch", "-q", "--prune", "--no-tags", "origin")
    guard = guard_path()
    if not guard:
        raise Refused("no pre-push guard: the wharf module's templates/code/git-hooks/pre-push is not installed "
                      "here — the clone would push unguarded")
    # The clone's own hooks folder: --git-path would follow a global core.hooksPath and overwrite
    # a shared pre-push, and with one set git would never run this one.
    if git(d, "config", "--get", "core.hooksPath", check=False).stdout.strip():
        raise Refused("core.hooksPath is set — the clone's pre-push guard would not run")
    hook = Path(git(d, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir").stdout.strip()) / "hooks" / "pre-push"
    hook.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    shutil.copyfile(guard, hook)
    hook.chmod(0o755)
    return d


def automerge_in(repo, ref):
    """The first file in ref that would let a PR merge itself, or None."""
    # -z: without it git quotes a non-ASCII path ("…mergé.yml") and the prefix check misses it.
    for n in filter(None, git(repo, "ls-tree", "-r", "-z", "--full-tree", "--name-only", ref).stdout.split("\0")):
        if n in MERGIFY:
            return n
        if n.startswith(".github/workflows/") and (
                AUTOMERGE.search(n) or AUTOMERGE.search(git(repo, "cat-file", "-p", f"{ref}:{n}").stdout)):
            return n
    return None


def gh(*args, cwd=None):
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["gh", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=cwd)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(["gh"], 127, "", "gh is not installed")


def pr_body(n_files, tree, parent, stat, removed, used, note, author_public=False):
    """What the PR says, built from the scan's verdict alone: everything in it is public."""
    per_file = {}
    for f, _s, c in used:
        per_file[f] = per_file.get(f, 0) + c
    out = [f"Publishes {n_files} allowlisted files from the private workshop as one commit on main "
           f"(tree {tree[:7]}). Merging this pull request is the publish; nothing has reached main yet.", ""]
    if note:
        out += [note.strip(), ""]
    out.append(f"Privacy scan (`helloworld`, {dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).date().isoformat()}): {n_files} files, the title, this "
               "body, the branch name, the commit identities"
               + (" (the PR's author is already public in this org)" if author_public else " and the PR's author")
               + "; 0 findings.")
    if per_file:
        out.append("Exemptions used (exact strings, each in its own file only): "
                   + ", ".join(f"{f} ×{c}" for f, c in sorted(per_file.items())) + ".")
    out += ["", f"Diff against main ({parent[:7]}):", "", "```"] + stat + ["```"]
    if removed:
        out += ["", f"Removes {removed} file{'s' if removed > 1 else ''} from main, not named here."]
    return "\n".join(out) + "\n"


def cmd_publish_pr(args):
    for v in ("GIT_DIR", "GIT_WORK_TREE", "GIT_COMMON_DIR"):
        if os.environ.get(v):  # it would point every clone call at the private repo
            raise Refused(f"{v} is set — the clone's git calls would act on another repo")
    b = build(args.repo, "origin/main", check_only=False)
    # The UTC date, as the commit's: a local date beside a UTC timestamp tells the time zone.
    today = dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).date().isoformat()
    title = args.message or f"Publish {today}"
    topic = re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9-]+", "-", (args.topic or "publish").lower()).strip("-") or "publish"
    slug = common.repo_slug(b["remote"])
    if not slug:
        raise Refused(f"{b['remote']} is not a GitHub URL — the pull request is opened with gh")
    tree = write_tree(b["repo"], b["paths"], b["files"])
    parent, refs = public_state(b["repo"], b["remote"])
    if not parent:
        raise Refused("the public repo has no main yet — a pull request needs one to merge into; "
                      "the first publish is `publish`, in the owner's terminal")
    # Auto-merge stays off (the owner, 2026-10-09) by refusal, not by absence: a ready claude/* PR lands
    # itself wherever an automerge workflow is on main, so neither the
    # public main nor the tree proposed for it may carry one.
    for where, ref in (("the public main", parent), ("this tree", tree)):
        n = automerge_in(b["repo"], ref)
        if n:
            raise Refused(f"{where} has {n} — a PR would merge itself; auto-merge stays off until the owner turns it on")
    stat = diff_lines(b, tree, parent)
    clone = public_clone(b)
    if git(clone, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "refs/remotes/origin/main", check=False).stdout.strip() != parent:
        raise Refused("the public main moved while this ran — run it again")
    branches = [l.split() for l in git(clone, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:lstrip=3) %(tree) %(objectname)",
                                        f"refs/remotes/origin/{PR_PREFIX.rstrip('/')}").stdout.splitlines() if l]
    # A proposal of this tree counts only on top of the current public main: a merged branch
    # nobody deleted must not block a take-back to its tree (review 2026-10-09).
    same = [n for n, t, c in branches
            if t == tree and git(clone, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", f"{c}^", check=False).stdout.strip() == parent
            # and only a signed one: a proposal the gate pushed before it signed fails the DCO check
            # for good, so a run replaces it rather than pointing at it (review 2026-10-10).
            and author_trailer(b["idents"]) in git(clone, "log", "-1", "--format=%B", c).stdout.splitlines()]
    # Files first, alone, so the body can count the exemptions they used.
    hits, used, bad_ex = scan(b["repo"], b["paths"], b["files"], b["ex"], b["words"])
    hits += history(b, refs)
    if stat is None or same:
        if not report(b, hits, used, bad_ex):
            return 1
        if stat is None:
            print("  nothing to propose — the public main already has exactly this tree. Nothing was pushed.")
            return 0
        # A branch with this tree is enough only while its PR is open: one whose PR was closed, or
        # whose `gh pr create` failed, would otherwise block this tree for good.
        for n in same:
            r = gh("pr", "list", "--repo", slug, "--head", n, "--state", "open", "--json", "url", "--jq", ".[].url")
            url = r.stdout.strip().splitlines()[0] if not r.returncode and r.stdout.strip() else ""
            if url:
                print(f"  nothing to propose — branch {n} already has exactly this tree, PR {url}. Nothing was pushed.")
                return 0
        why = "has no open PR" if not r.returncode else f"could not be checked for a PR: {(r.stderr or '').strip()[:200]}"
        print(f"  branch {same[0]} already has exactly this tree but {why}. Nothing was pushed.\n"
              f"  open it with: gh pr create --repo {slug} --base main --head {same[0]}")
        return 2
    taken = {n for n, _t, _c in branches}
    branch, k = f"{PR_PREFIX}{today}-{topic}", 2
    while branch in taken:
        branch, k = f"{PR_PREFIX}{today}-{topic}-{k}", k + 1
    idents = b["idents"]
    # The body's diff-stat leaves out removed files: their names are already public, and a
    # take-back that drops a leaked file must not be refused for naming it (review 2026-10-09).
    # --no-renames: a rename would print `old => new` and name the old path after all.
    body_stat = git(b["repo"], "diff", "--stat", "--no-renames", "--diff-filter=d", parent, tree).stdout.rstrip().splitlines()
    removed = len(git(b["repo"], "diff", "--name-only", "--no-renames", "-z", "--diff-filter=D", parent, tree)
                  .stdout.split("\0")) - 1
    # The PR's author is the gh login, shown on the public PR: a machine account is a private word.
    # Unread, it is unscanned, so nothing goes up (fails closed; an early version skipped it and pushed).
    r = gh("api", "user", "--jq", ".login")
    if r.returncode or not r.stdout.strip():
        raise Refused("cannot read the gh login the PR would show, so it cannot be scanned; nothing was pushed "
                      f"({(r.stderr or 'no login').strip()[:200]})")
    login = r.stdout.strip()
    # A login that already authored a merged pull request in a public repo of the target owner is
    # public already (the owner's own first PR on a twin), so it passes even when the word list
    # holds it to catch private repo paths like <login>/pipeline (2026-10-10: a first publish
    # refused the owner's own login). Not org membership: the owner's is private, and the org's
    # public_members was empty (probed). `is:public` matters: the token sees private
    # repos too; `org:` matches a user-owned namespace as well (probed). The machine account has no
    # such PR (probed: 0), so it stays refused. Searched only for a login on the word list (the
    # search API's limit is small), and only a well-formed login, whose own name the result must
    # carry. A failed search prints its error body on stdout, so only its exit code is trusted; it
    # then scans the login as before (fails closed).
    words_rx, public_as = words_regex(b["words"]), ""
    if any(rx.search(login) for rx in words_rx) and re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9-]+", login):
        r = gh("api", "-X", "GET", "search/issues", "-f",
               f"q=is:pr is:merged is:public author:{login} org:{slug.split('/')[0]}",
               "--jq", f'[.items[] | select(.user.login == "{login}")][0].html_url // empty')
        public_as = r.stdout.strip() if not r.returncode else ""
        if r.returncode:
            print(f"  PR author: the search for a public merged PR failed, so the login is scanned "
                  f"({(r.stderr or 'no answer').strip()[:200]})")
    body = pr_body(len(b["paths"]), tree, parent, body_stat, removed, used, args.note, bool(public_as))
    # The identities' address is the one the owner chose to publish (as publish's): the address
    # shape is not a finding there, a private word (a machine, an account) is.
    meta = [("PR title", title, ()), ("PR body", body, ()), ("PR branch", branch, ())]
    meta += [(f"commit {k}", who, {"e-mail address"}) for k, who in idents]
    # The commit's Signed-off-by trailer repeats the author identity (the address is the owner's
    # chosen one, as above); scanned as written so the message that goes public is covered whole.
    meta.append(("commit trailer", author_trailer(idents) or "", {"e-mail address"}))
    if public_as:
        print(mask_words(f"  PR author: {login}, already public as the author of {public_as}", words_rx))
    else:
        meta.append(("PR author (gh login)", login, ()))
    # Prevention, beside the scan: a squash or merge commit is authored by GitHub with the merging
    # account's own address (the 220-film incident); a rebase merge keeps the commit's author. The
    # repo's settings must say rebase only, or the owner's merge would be the next leak. Unread is refused.
    r = gh("api", f"repos/{slug}", "--jq", "[.allow_squash_merge, .allow_merge_commit] | map(tostring) | join(\" \")")
    state = r.stdout.split() if not r.returncode else []
    if state != ["false", "false"]:
        why = ("allow_squash_merge or allow_merge_commit is on" if "true" in state else
               f"the merge settings could not be read ({(r.stderr or 'no answer').strip()[:200]})")
        raise Refused(f"{slug}: {why} — a squash or merge commit would be authored by the merging account's own "
                      f"address. Set rebase-only first: gh api -X PATCH repos/{slug} -F allow_squash_merge=false "
                      "-F allow_merge_commit=false. Nothing was pushed.")
    hits += scan(b["repo"], [], b["files"], [], b["words"], meta=meta)[0]
    if not report(b, hits, used, bad_ex):
        print("  Nothing was pushed.")
        return 1
    print(f"  diff against the public main ({parent[:7]}):")
    print("\n".join(mask_words("    " + l, words_regex(b["words"])) for l in stat))
    print(f"  commit: \"{title}\", a child of {parent[:7]}, dated in UTC")
    for k, who in idents:
        print(f"    {k}: {who}")
    print(f"    trailer: {author_trailer(idents) or 'none — git has no author identity, so the commit will fail'}")
    utc = f"{int(time.time())} +0000"
    sha = git(b["repo"], "commit-tree", tree, "-p", parent, "-m", signed_off(title, idents),
              env={"GIT_AUTHOR_DATE": utc, "GIT_COMMITTER_DATE": utc}).stdout.strip()
    # The clone fetches the commit by a ref of the gate's own, which lives for this fetch only.
    git(b["repo"], "update-ref", PR_REF, sha)
    try:
        git(clone, "fetch", "-q", "--no-tags", str(b["repo"]), f"+{PR_REF}:refs/heads/{branch}")
    finally:
        git(b["repo"], "update-ref", "-d", PR_REF, check=False)
    if git(clone, "rev-parse", f"refs/heads/{branch}").stdout.strip() != sha:
        raise Refused(f"the clone's {branch} is not {sha[:7]} after the fetch")
    # GIT_PUSH_OK and VS Code's marker stripped: the guard decides, and it lets claude/* through
    # and nothing else.
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k not in ("GIT_PUSH_OK", "VSCODE_GIT_COMMAND")}
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(clone), "push", "-q", "origin", f"refs/heads/{branch}:refs/heads/{branch}"],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, env=dict(env, GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT="0"))
    if r.returncode:
        raise Refused(f"push of {branch} refused: {(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[:300]}")
    seen = git(clone, "ls-remote", "origin", f"refs/heads/{branch}").stdout.split()
    if not seen or seen[0] != sha:
        print(f"Pushed, but the public {branch} reads {seen[0][:7] if seen else 'nothing'}, not {sha[:7]} — "
              "look before opening a PR.")
        return 2
    with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile("w", suffix=".md", delete=False) as fh:
        fh.write(body)
    try:
        # No --draft (a draft is as public as a PR, and the owner's merge is the gate) and no --auto:
        # auto-merge stays off (the owner, 2026-10-09).
        r = gh("pr", "create", "--repo", slug, "--base", "main", "--head", branch,
               "--title", title, "--body-file", fh.name, cwd=str(clone))
    finally:
        os.unlink(fh.name)
    if r.returncode:
        print(f"Pushed {branch} ({sha[:7]}, read back), but no PR: {(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[:300]}\n"
              f"  open it with: gh pr create --repo {slug} --base main --head {branch}")
        return 2
    url = (r.stdout.strip().splitlines() or [""])[-1]
    log_row(b["repo"], f"- {today} pr {branch} {sha} {url}")
    print(f"Proposed {sha[:7]} as {branch} (read back): {url}\n"
          f"  Merging it is the publish; the public main is untouched until then. Logged in {log_path(b['repo'])}.")
    return 0


def main(argv=None):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="helloworld", description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="verb", required=True)
    c = sub.add_parser("check", help="build and scan; never pushes")
    c.add_argument("repo")
    c.add_argument("--ref", default="origin/main", help="the commit to build from (default: origin/main, fetched)")
    p = sub.add_parser("publish", help="check origin/main, then commit and push (the owner's terminal only); "
                                       "--pr: a pull request instead (an agent may run it)")
    p.add_argument("repo")
    p.add_argument("--pr", action="store_true", help="push a claude/publish/* branch and open a PR; never main")
    p.add_argument("-m", "--message", help="the public commit's message, and with --pr the PR's title "
                                           "(default: Published <date>; with --pr, Publish <date>)")
    p.add_argument("--topic", help="--pr: the branch's topic (default: publish); scanned")
    p.add_argument("--note", help="--pr: a paragraph for the PR body — what this publish carries; scanned")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if args.verb == "publish" and not args.pr and (args.topic or args.note):
        ap.error("--topic and --note go with --pr")
    try:
        if args.verb == "check":
            return cmd_check(args)
        return cmd_publish_pr(args) if args.pr else cmd_publish(args)
    except Refused as e:
        print(f"helloworld {args.verb}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
